    "content": "<div class=\"context-box\">\n<h2 class=\"context-title\">The World That Raised Him: 1952–1964</h2>\n<p class=\"context-intro\">In the years between Bob's birth and the dawn of his adolescence, America underwent a transformation more profound than any since the Civil War. The world he entered in 1952 was still emerging from the shadow of global conflict, but the seeds of the modern age were already germinating in ways that would define the rest of the century.</p>\n\n<p>When Bob was born in the fall of 1952, Dwight D. Eisenhower was campaigning for the presidency on a promise to end the Korean War, television was a luxury fewer than one-third of American households owned, and the nation's interstate highway system existed only as a dream on drawing boards. Sputnik, the event that would ignite Bob's imagination and set his life on a trajectory toward the stars, was still five years in the future. The Cold War had settled into its familiar rhythms — nuclear anxieties that came in waves, air raid drills that children treated as games, and the quiet assumption that America's technological supremacy was both natural and permanent.</p>\n\n<p>It was a time of rigid social structures and rapidly shifting foundations. The postwar baby boom was swelling elementary schools across the Midwest. Suburbs were spreading across former farmland at a pace that alarmed conservationists and delighted homebuilders. The automobiles Bob's father worked on were still largely built by hand, with chrome fins growing larger each year, and gasoline cost twenty cents a gallon. The polio vaccine had just arrived, erasing a terror that had haunted every summer of every parent's life for decades.</p>\n\n<p>In Northern Michigan, where Bob's first memories formed, the old world lingered longer than in the cities. Cabins lacked central heating. Roads turned to mud in the spring thaw. Families gathered around radios rather than television sets, and the news of the wider world arrived filtered through the voices of broadcasters who sounded like they were speaking from another century. The 1950s are often remembered as a golden age of American prosperity, and for many white middle-class families they were exactly that. But the era also contained its own tensions — McCarthy's witch hunts, the rumblings of a civil rights movement that would soon explode, and the growing awareness that the nuclear umbrella protecting the American way of life was more fragile than anyone wanted to admit.</p>\n\n<p>By the time Bob reached school age, the world had already begun to accelerate. The launch of Sputnik in 1957, which he heard as a five-year-old crouched by his grandparents' radio, triggered a national crisis of confidence that reshaped American education, science funding, and ultimately the trajectory of his own life. The Space Race became a national obsession, and a boy who had learned electronics by fixing broken televisions in his father's garage would find himself, against all odds, at the center of humanity's reach for the stars.</p>\n\n<p>These were the years that molded him — not through grand events alone, but through the texture of ordinary life in a time of extraordinary change. The smell of a cabin in summer. The crackle of a radio console. The weight of a hand on his shoulder as a grandfather spoke of knights and builders. The child who touched an electrical outlet in a Michigan kitchen was also a child of the Cold War, the Space Age, and the American Century — and all of those forces were already at work, shaping the man he would become.</p>\n</div>"
