  "part2": {
    "title": "The Crucible of Change: 1964–1972",
    "content": "<div class=\"context-box\">\n<h2 class=\"context-title\">The Crucible of Change: 1964–1972</h2>\n<p class=\"context-intro\">The years of Bob's adolescence and young adulthood were among the most turbulent in American history — a period when the certainties of the 1950s shattered and a radically different nation emerged from the wreckage. For a boy in Michigan with his hands covered in sawdust and solder, the world outside the garage was transforming at a pace that must have felt, at times, impossible to keep pace with.</p>\n\n<p>By the time Bob entered high school in the mid-1960s, the postwar consensus had collapsed. The assassination of John F. Kennedy in 1963 had cracked something fundamental in the American psyche — a sense that history's arc bent toward progress, that the worst was behind them. What followed was a cascade of events that rewrote the nation's script: the escalation of the Vietnam War under Lyndon Johnson, the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965, the rise of the counterculture, the assassinations of Martin Luther King Jr. and Robert Kennedy in 1968, and the riots that consumed American cities in the summers of those years.</p>\n\n<p>The Vietnam War, which loomed over Bob's senior year when his draft number came up low, was the defining trauma of his generation. It was a war fought not by a mobilized nation but by a lottery, a conflict that fell unevenly on the working class and the poor. The draft deferment Bob received through his electronics training was not heroism — it was survival, a practical decision made by a young man who understood that the alternative meant rice paddies and body bags. More than one hundred thousand draft-age Americans fled to Canada. Thousands more went to prison. The fifty-eight thousand names on the Vietnam Memorial in Washington represent only the dead; the wounded — physically and psychologically — numbered in the millions.</p>\n\n<p>But the decade was not defined by war alone. It was also a time of extraordinary technological acceleration. Integrated circuits, invented in 1958, were beginning to transform every industry. The minicomputer revolution made computing accessible to universities and businesses for the first time. When Bob encountered the PDP-8 at Michigan State, he was touching the leading edge of a wave that would crest into the personal computer revolution of the 1970s and 80s. The Apollo program reached its zenith with the moon landing in 1969, fulfilling the promise John F. Kennedy had made at the dawn of the decade. Bob watched Neil Armstrong step onto the lunar surface, and he understood — perhaps for the first time — that the skills he was developing in electronics labs and physics classrooms could connect him to something genuinely historic.</p>\n\n<p>In Michigan, the automobile industry that had built the state's prosperity was beginning its long decline, though few recognized it yet. The Great Lakes still teemed with fish, the forests still stretched green to the horizon, and the seasons still turned with the dependable rhythm that had governed the region for millennia. But beneath the surface, the Vietnam draft, the civil rights movement, the environmental awakening, and the technological revolution were reshaping the landscape in ways that would take decades to fully understand.</p>\n\n<p>Bob built a boat in his father's garage during these years. It was an act of creation in a time of destruction — a small rebellion against the chaos of the era, a reminder that building things mattered even when the world seemed determined to tear itself apart. He did not know it then, but the discipline he learned in that garage — measuring twice, cutting once, persisting through failure — would carry him through everything that followed.</p>\n</div>"
