    "content": "<div class=\"context-box\">\n<h2 class=\"context-title\">The Age of Discovery: 1972–1999</h2>\n<p class=\"context-intro\">The quarter-century between Bob's early career at Fermilab and the dawn of the new millennium was arguably the most technologically transformative period in human history. When Bob began his professional life in 1970, a computer filled a room, long-distance phone calls required an operator, and the idea of a global information network existed only in the minds of a few visionaries. By 1999, those same technologies had been woven into the fabric of everyday life, and the world stood on the threshold of changes that would make even the twentieth century seem quaint.</p>\n\n<p>Bob's years at Fermilab coincided with one of the golden ages of American physics. The laboratory's main accelerator, then the most powerful in the world, was probing the fundamental structure of matter with a precision that would have seemed miraculous to the physicists of the previous generation. In 1977, Fermilab scientists discovered the bottom quark, a finding that confirmed the Standard Model of particle physics and earned a Nobel Prize. The work Bob did there — building instruments to measure the energy of subatomic particles — placed him at the intersection of pure science and practical engineering, a position that would define his entire career.</p>\n\n<p>The 1970s were a time of economic uncertainty — oil crises, stagflation, the end of the postwar boom — but for someone with Bob's skills, opportunity abounded. The aerospace industry was expanding, driven by Cold War imperatives and the legacy of the Apollo program. When Bob moved from Fermilab to flight test engineering at Eglin Air Force Base, he entered a world where the boundaries of what was possible were being pushed daily — not in the abstract realm of quarks and quantum fields, but in the concrete reality of aircraft that pushed the edges of speed, altitude, and maneuverability.</p>\n\n<p>The personal computer revolution of the late 1970s and 1980s caught most of the world by surprise. Bob, with his background in both hardware and software, was better positioned than most to understand what was coming. The Apple II (1977), the IBM PC (1981), and the Macintosh (1984) transformed computing from a specialized tool for scientists and businesses into something approaching a mass-market consumer product. The internet, which began as ARPANET in 1969, evolved through the 1980s and exploded into public consciousness in the mid-1990s with the World Wide Web. By 1999, the dot-com boom was in full swing — a speculative frenzy that would crash in 2000 but that, in its exuberance, laid the infrastructure for the connected world of the twenty-first century.</p>\n\n<p>Y2K, which was Bob's last major professional challenge before the new millennium, was a collective achievement of the technology industry working together across companies and countries. An estimated $300 billion was spent worldwide on Y2K remediation. The fact that the transition happened without catastrophe — that the power stayed on, the planes stayed in the air, and the bank accounts remained intact — was not an accident. It was the result of millions of engineers doing thankless work, tracing ancient code, testing systems, and preparing for a crisis that, because of their efforts, never materialized. It was, in many ways, the profession's finest hour — and Bob was there for it.</p>\n\n<p>But the personal history of these years — marriage, children, illness, loss, the slow accumulation of joy and grief that constitutes a life — cannot be separated from the historical currents around them. Nancy's cancer was not a product of its era, but the way they fought it was. The treatments available, the clinical trials Bob researched, the insurance system that capped their benefits, the financial devastation that followed — all of these were the products of a particular time and place in American medicine. Bob's technical skills, honed over decades of engineering practice, became tools for navigating a crisis that no amount of engineering could solve. It is one of the bitter ironies of life that the skills we develop for one purpose often prove most valuable for entirely different ones.</p>\n\n<p>The millennium arrived. The computers did not crash. Bob became a widower. And the world, indifferent to both, continued turning into a new century that would bring its own challenges, its own wonders, its own unbearable losses and unexpected gifts.</p>\n</div>"
