  "part4": {
    "title": "The New Century: 2000–2024",
    "content": "<div class=\"context-box\">\n<h2 class=\"context-title\">The New Century: 2000–2024</h2>\n<p class=\"context-intro\">The first quarter of the twenty-first century was a time of dizzying acceleration and profound disruption — an era when the digital infrastructure Bob helped build transformed every aspect of human life, and when the fragility of that progress became impossible to ignore. For a man entering his fifties, then sixties, then seventies, the pace of change was something to be navigated, not resisted — a current that could carry you forward if you learned to swim with it.</p>\n\n<p>The new millennium did not, as expected, begin quietly. The dot-com crash of 2000 wiped out trillions in market value but left the internet's physical infrastructure intact — fiber optic cables, server farms, and the protocols that would power the next wave. Then came September 11, 2001, an event that reshaped global politics, security, and daily life in ways that are still unfolding. The wars in Afghanistan and Iraq that followed consumed two decades and trillions of dollars, leaving a legacy of instability and trauma that future generations will inherit.</p>\n\n<p>Through it all, the technological revolution accelerated. The smartphone, introduced by Apple in 2007, put more computing power in people's pockets than Bob's PDP-8 had delivered to an entire university. Social media platforms rewired human communication, for better and worse. Cloud computing, big data, and machine learning transformed industries at a pace that made the changes of the 1990s look incremental. Bob, never one to retreat from new technology, embraced the AI revolution that began in earnest in the 2020s. He experimented with language models, prompt engineering, and generative AI — not as a passive consumer, but as someone who had spent a lifetime understanding systems and wanted to understand this one too.</p>\n\n<p>The second half of Bob's story in this period was shaped by two forces: the slow process of rebuilding after loss, and the discovery that joy could return even when you had stopped expecting it. Cindy entered his life at a time when he thought the chapter of romantic partnership was permanently closed. Their marriage was a testament to the truth that some of life's best gifts arrive after you have stopped looking for them — and that the capacity to love does not diminish with age but changes shape, becoming something quieter and perhaps deeper.</p>\n\n<p>The grandchildren arrived, one after another, filling Bob's later years with the particular sweetness of watching new generations discover the world. The family grew in ways he had not anticipated — stepchildren and step-grandchildren blending into the mix, creating a network of connection that spanned biology and choice. The 3D printer on his desk, humming away at some small plastic object, was both a tool and a symbol: the old engineer, still making things, still learning, still curious.</p>\n\n<p>The world beyond his door was not as kind. The COVID-19 pandemic of 2020–2023 killed millions globally and exposed deep fragilities in healthcare systems, supply chains, and social cohesion. Climate change, once a distant threat, became an immediate reality — wildfires, floods, heat waves, and storms that broke records with alarming regularity. Political divisions deepened in ways that seemed to threaten the democratic institutions Bob had taken for granted throughout his life. The future, which had once seemed like a place of promise, felt increasingly uncertain.</p>\n\n<p>Bob had lived through uncertainty before. He had watched his wife die. He had watched his daughter nearly die. He had seen wars, economic collapses, technological revolutions, and cultural transformations that would have seemed impossible to the boy in the Michigan cabin. What he had learned — what age had taught him, if nothing else — was that the world keeps turning, that human beings are more resilient than they know, and that the only reliable response to uncertainty is to keep building. To keep loving. To keep showing up.</p>\n\n<p>Even when you do not know what comes next.</p>\n</div>"
  },
  "part5": {
    "title": "The Unwritten Century: 2025–2100",
    "content": "<div class=\"context-box\">\n<h2 class=\"context-title\">The Unwritten Century: 2025–2100</h2>\n<p class=\"context-intro\">What follows is speculation — an imagining of the world Bob's descendants will inherit, based on the seeds already planted in the present. Some of these predictions will be wrong; history has a way of surprising even the most careful forecasters. But they represent a plausible trajectory, shaped by the forces already visible on the horizon.</p>\n\n<p>The middle decades of the twenty-first century will be defined by the full flowering of artificial intelligence — not merely as a tool, but as a transformative force that reshapes work, creativity, governance, and human relationships. By 2050, the debate will no longer be about whether machines can think, but about what kind of thinking we want machines to do, and what we want to preserve for ourselves. The systems Bob experimented with in his later years will have evolved into something that earlier generations could not have recognized — distributed, adaptive, and in some cases, arguably conscious. The question of rights for artificial minds will be one of the defining moral debates of the era, as profound as the abolition of slavery or the extension of suffrage.</p>\n\n<p>Climate change will have remapped the planet by mid-century. Coastal cities will have invested billions in adaptation — sea walls, floating districts, pumped drainage systems — but some inhabited places will have been abandoned. Migrations driven by heat, drought, and rising seas will have redrawn political boundaries and tested social cohesion. The countries that manage these transitions well will emerge stronger; those that resist will face collapse. Michigan, with its Great Lakes holding one-fifth of the world's fresh surface water, will become a destination for climate refugees — a reversal of the twentieth-century pattern of migration from the Rust Belt to the Sun Belt.</p>\n\n<p>Space will have become a domain of permanent human presence, though not the romantic frontier of twentieth-century imagination. The Moon will host research stations, manufacturing facilities, and perhaps the first off-world settlements. Mars will have been reached but not yet tamed — a destination for the dedicated and the desperate, not for tourists. The first child born off Earth will arrive in the 2040s or 2050s, and their existence will change the human relationship with the cosmos in ways that are difficult to predict. They will be the first true extraterrestrials — humans who have never felt rain or wind or the pull of Earth's full gravity, whose baseline reality is the inside of a pressurized habitat.</p>\n\n<p>Warfare will have changed more fundamentally than at any time since the nuclear age. Autonomous weapons — swarms of small drones, AI-directed missiles, cyber attacks on infrastructure — will have made traditional armies nearly obsolete and simultaneously more dangerous. The threshold for conflict will be lower when no human soldier needs to cross a border. The ethical frameworks for this new kind of warfare will lag behind the technology, as they always do, and the consequences will be paid in blood by those caught between systems that follow their programming to its logical conclusion.</p>\n\n<p>Medicine will have made progress that seems miraculous by early-century standards. Genetic therapies, nanomedicine, and AI-driven diagnosis will have extended healthy lifespans and eliminated some diseases that plagued humanity for millennia. But these advances will be distributed unevenly, creating new forms of inequality between those who can afford biological enhancement and those who cannot. The grandchildren of Bob's grandchildren may face choices about their own biology that would have seemed like science fiction to their great-grandfather — and they will have to navigate those choices without moral maps from earlier eras.</p>\n\n<p>The most important changes, however, may be invisible. The way people think about identity, community, time, and meaning will shift in response to technologies that restructure attention and relationship. The concept of a single, stable career — already eroded in Bob's time — will seem as archaic as the idea of a single, stable nation-state. People will form communities across vast distances through immersive technologies that make physical presence feel less essential. The boundaries between human and machine, between self and network, between private and public, will blur in ways that create both liberation and disorientation.</p>\n\n<p>Bob will not see any of this. He knows that. But the skills he learned — curiosity, persistence, the willingness to build in the face of uncertainty — will be more valuable in this strange new century than any specific knowledge he could have passed down. The tools change. The questions remain. What does it mean to live well? To love well? To leave the world better than you found it? These questions are not answered by technology but by the human heart that uses it. And that, ultimately, is what Bob hopes his descendants will remember.</p>\n</div>"
  }
